ID:TB Taming Keyboard Polling Programs
Quarterdeck Technical Note #188                       Filename: TAMEBYTE.TEC
by Stafford "Doc" Williamson                        CompuServe: TAMEBT.TEC
Last revised:  10/12/93                               Category: DV

Subject:  How to improve the performance of your system when a program
          running in background under DESQview or DESQview/X constantly
          "polls" the keyboard, noticeably slowing down your foreground
          application.


WHEN I RUN A CERTAIN PROGRAM IN THE BACKGROUND UNDER DESQview (OR DESQview/X)
MY FOREGROUND APPLICATION SLOWS TO A CRAWL.  WHAT CAN I DO?


Many DOS programs are written as if they will be the only program running on
your PC.  This assumption leads to problems when such programs are run in a
multitasking environment such as DESQview or DESQview/X.  A program may
perform its tasks in a way that is efficient when no other programs are
executing but can interfere with other programs when run under a multitasker.
One such task that some programs perform is "keyboard polling."

When writing the code that comprises his program, a programmer can use a
number of methods to determine whether his program has been sent a command
that requires its attention.  He can have the program issue a pause, causing
it to wait for input in the form of an interrupt request from the keyboard.
Alternatively, the program can be instructed to continuously poll the keyboard
buffer in search of incoming keystrokes.  As this second method tends to be
somewhat faster, it is often the method of choice and can be implemented
through the user of a "program loop."  A program loop might contain code
similar to this:

        10    check keyboard buffer
        20    if keystrokes received
        30    do process keystrokes
        40    else  GOTO 10     ; start this process again

If such a program is running in the background under DESQview or DESQview/X,
significant amounts of processor time will be wasted while it waits for input
from the keyboard, noticeably slowing down the performance of any program
running in foreground.

This technote will discuss three methods you can use to improve the
performance of your system when running such programs.  The first method can
be used with DESQview but NOT with DESQview/X.  The second and third methods
are applicable to both programs.


1.  SET "RUNS IN BACKGROUND" TO NO.

Each program that is run from the DESQview menu has an associated file with
the extension ".DVP".  .DVP files contain configuration information about your
programs, such as the keys used to start them from the DV menu and the amount
of memory each needs to run.  The .DVP is edited by choosing "OPEN" from the
DV menu, typing "CP" (for Change a Program) and then selecting the program you
want to change.  This brings up the first of two screens, "Standard Options."
Use the F10 key to bring up the second screen, "Advanced Options."

Set "Runs in background (Y,N,blank)" to "N" (for NO) and then hit ENTER to
save the change.  Now this program will neither run nor take any CPU time
unless it is the foreground application.  (Please note that a "foreground"
application is the active window which is accepting input from the keyboard.
Any program other than the foreground application is said to be running in
background whether or not it is visible on your screen.)

As noted above, this option is ONLY available for classic, text-based
DESQview, not for DESQview/X.  Further, if your program must be allowed to run
in background this option is not a valid one.


2.  EDITING THE .DVP FILE WITH A HEX EDITOR.

If you have a HEX editor (such as those included with Norton Utilities or
XTGold) and are comfortable using it, you can alter the programming code of a
.DVP file to limit the number of keyboard polling loops a program is allowed
to make before DESQview or DESQview/X forces the application to give up the
rest of its processor time-slice.

Before you attempt to alter a .DVP file ALWAYS MAKE A BACK-UP COPY OF THE
FILE.  If you inadvertently change the wrong byte, your program may not run at
all.   As long as you have made a copy of the .DVP you can easily restore the
file to its previous state.

After backing up the file, load it into your HEX editor.  The following
example shows a DESQview .DVP called AC-PIF.DVP loaded into XTGold's editor:

 000070   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000080   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000090   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 0000A0   20 20 20 20  20 00 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 0000B0   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 0000C0   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 0000D0   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 0000E0   20 20 20 20  20 7F 01 00  FF 19 50 07  0E 16 00 00
 0000F0   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000100   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000110   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000120   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 00
 000130   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000140   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000150   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20
 000160   20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 20  20 20 20 21
 000170   20 41 43 2A  04 00 00 01  01 01 01 01  01 00 02 00
 000180   08 37 00 00  2C 00 00 11  00 FF 00 43  02 00 00 00
 000190   00 00 00 00  00 00 00 00  00 00 00 00  00 00 00 00

The byte we want to change is 175.  Find the line which begins 000170.   In
this example byte 170 is represented by the first two digits following 000170,
or 20; byte 171 is 41; byte 172 is 43; byte 173 is 2A; byte 174 is 04; and
byte 175 is 00.  It is this 00 that must be altered.

First, keep in mind that you must replace 00 with a two digit number in HEX.
For instance, the decimal 1 is written "01" in HEX, which seems reasonable
even to those unfamiliar with hexadecimal.  However, the decimal 11 is written
"0b" in hex, which is not as obvious.  If you do not know how to convert
decimal numbers to hexadecimal, you can download a small (3K) file from the
Quarterdeck BBS called DVASCII.ZIP.  This file, when unzipped, gives you a
DV-specific chart of ASCII codes listing the HEX equivalents of decimal
numbers from 1 to 255--more than enough for our purposes.   You may also want
to download HEX.TEC, our technical bulletin which explains hexadecimal.  You
can dial into the BBS by calling (310) 314-3227 from your modem, using
settings 8/N/1.

Additionally, you may need to experiment at length to find the number that
provides the best overall performance.  Some programs continuously poll the
keyboard when not performing any other tasks. Changing byte 175 to a decimal
value of 1 (or HEX 01) may improve the performance of a second program running
in foreground.  However, some programs intermittently poll the keyboard
between tasks.  Setting such a program to a decimal value of 1 will interfere
with the program's ability to accomplish its work, degrading its performance.
It is up to you to find an appropriate value that makes all of your programs
run well together.

After editing the .DVP file, you should run the program in background and in
foreground and in combination with various other programs to test the new
value.  If you are not happy with the results, try another value.


3.  USE "TAME" BY POWERSOFT, INC.

PowerSoft, Inc. sells a "multi-tasker tamer" called TAME.  For information on
this product, contact PowerSoft at (404) 516-0759 (voice),
(404) 516-0381 (FAX), or (404) 928-9294 (BBS).


ARE THERE PROGRAMS THAT CANNOT BE "TAMED" BY THESE LAST TWO TECHNIQUES?

Folio Corporation's "Previews" and "Views" do not appear to be helped by
PowerSoft's TAME or by altering the .DVP file.  PowerSoft and Folio Corp. are
looking into this problem.

If you know of any other programs that cannot be "tamed" by one of these
methods, we would like to hear about them.

